  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe current approach relies on local optimization methods that respect the integral constraint. We propose an enhanced metaheuristic based on guided construction and curriculum learning to systematically explore the solution space. This acknowledges that:\n   - The optimization problem is highly nonconvex with many local minima\n   - Global search requires structural decisions\n   - Progressive difficulty and random choices need guidance\nThe central idea is to use a hierarchy of geometric structures - from circular plaque-like patterns to fractal-inspired morphologies, checking mathematical validity and overlap conditions at each stage.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nThe primary variables are:\n- h[i] \u220a [0,1] for i=0..N-1\n- Total integral C5 = dx \u00d7 max_lag \u27e8h,1-h\u27e9\n- Convergence criterion: c5_improvement < 1e-14 or time > budget\n\nFor each curriculum stage:\n- Stage 0: Verify input constraints\n- Stage 1: Generate binary plaque kernel\n- Stage 2: Density modulation with Chebyshev series\n- Stage 3: Fractal expansion with simple correlation checks\n- Stage 4: Local deformation\n- Validation: compute exact overlap using finite element method on the discrete grid\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nGENERATE_STRUCTURE(SEED, N_POINTS, SCALE, TYPE ):\n  switch(TYPE):\n    case 'PLAQUE': circular symmetric with randomized edge\n    case 'DIAMOND': four-point symmetric pattern\n    case 'SQUARE': lattice based with diagonal preference\n    case 'SEMI_CIRC': semi-annular then fractal refinement\n  Return kernel\n\nMODULATE(KERNEL, N_POINTS, BANDWIDTH):\n  Project onto [0,2] grid\n  Apply Chebyshev filter with bandwidth\n  Project with clipping to satisfy integral\n\nOPTIMIZE_STRUCTURE(init, N_POINTS, max_iter, pop_size=50):\n  Binary encoding\n  Custom crossover focusing on shift domains\n  Elitist selection penalizing high max_overlap\n  Mutation based on nearest neighbor pattern borrowing\n\nPHASE1: Structured candidate generation\n  For m in range(curriculum):\n      Style = ['PLAQUE', 'DIAMOND', 'SQUARE'][m%3]\n      Kernel = GENERATE_STRUCTURE(m, N_POINTS, 0.7, Style)\n      Candidate = MODULATE(Kernel, N_POINTS, 0.1 + 0.2*m/10)\n      Deform_candidate = OPTIMIZE_STRUCTURE(Candidate, ...)\n      Record = compute_c5(Deformed)\n      Compare with record\n\nPHASE2: Fractal morphing\n  Start from best_phase1 solution\n  Seven fractal types \u03b1=0..6\n  Apply recursive morphing\n  Fast pruning of degenerate cases\n\n4. Search choices\n- Evaluate 6 curriculum types per phase\n- Each curriculum type runs for 240 seconds\n- Gradient replacement after 20 failures\n- Best 5 of each type are saved\n- Restart with winnning patterns if improvement stalls\n\n5. Budget\n- Curriculum phase: 12 realizations\n- Each phase: 120 seconds wall time + adaptive idle\n- Total: 1440 seconds\n- Check feasibility and record after every deformation\n- Stop after the first improvement greater than -0.0005\n\n6. Validation\nApply finite element computation of overlap\nCheck \u2211h_i * dx \u2248 1 exactly\nReject any solution that fails periodicity check\nFinal selection by mathematical proof of optimal packing in overlap space",
